
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">&#8220;The key question is not whether AI is used, but how it is used.&#8221; That&#8217;s from Springer Nature&#8217;s <a href="https://www.springernature.com/gp/policies/editorial-policies/using-ai-in-peer-review" data-type="link" data-id="https://www.springernature.com/gp/policies/editorial-policies/using-ai-in-peer-review" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">policy</a> on using AI when peer reviewing for its journals.</p>



<span id="more-26326"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wiley <a href="https://www.wiley.com/publishing/article/ai-guidelines#peerreview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">tells</a> its journal referees: &#8220;Use AI responsibly: Apply best practices and properly disclose AI use in manuscripts and peer review.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That&#8217;s right: two of the largest academic publishers allow referees to use AI when reviewing submissions to their journals. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Springer has created a &#8220;risk-assessment framework for AI use in peer review&#8221; to provide some further guidance:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="630" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9-1024x630.png" alt="" class="wp-image-26330" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9-1024x630.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9-300x185.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9-768x473.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9-1536x945.png 1536w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9-250x154.png 250w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Springer-AI-peer-review-risk-chart-2026-9.png 1857w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Springer Nature&#8217;s &#8220;AI Risk Assessment Framework for AI Use in Peer Review&#8221;</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wiley provides fewer details, saying only that &#8221; Manuscripts under review are confidential and must not be uploaded to AI Technologies, whether in full or in part.&#8221; But it seems have a workaround for this in development&#8212;at least that&#8217;s how I interpret them following a brief list of what editors and reviewers can &#8220;do safely&#8221; with AI with the announcement that they&#8217;re  &#8220;developing new approaches that will expand these possibilities while maintaining the confidentiality expected in review. &#8220;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Oxford University Press <a href="https://academic.oup.com/pages/for-authors/books/author-use-of-artificial-intelligence" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">says</a>: &#8220;OUP selects peer reviewers for their expertise in the field and requires them to evaluate content based on their expert judgement alone. It is prohibited to upload project proposals and manuscripts, in part or in whole, into a Gen AI tool for any purpose.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Meanwhile, <a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/resource/ai_guidelines.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">the policy</a> of University of Chicago Press on AI use by referees is more restrictive than the others above. It says: </p>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1270b9d1 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained" style="padding-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--50);padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--50)">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>In peer review, we require unassisted evaluations and expressly prohibit the use of AI tools in writing readers’ reports. Out of respect for the author’s intellectual property, peer reviewers should not copy any portion of a manuscript or a proposal, nor the reader’s report itself, into an AI tool, even one that promises not to retain data.</em></p>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some journals have their own policies regarding the use of AI by referees. <em>Ergo</em>,  for instance, says: </p>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1270b9d1 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained" style="padding-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--50);padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--50)">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph" style="margin-right:0;margin-left:0"><em>We require our referees not to use AI in any form for reviewing of articles. In particular, referees must not enter any article text into AI systems, including AI checking tools such as Pangram. If referees have concerns about the use of AI in submitted articles, they should instead raise these concerns directly with the area editor. </em></p>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">But many journals lack such a policy, and don&#8217;t say anything explicit in their own guidelines or description of their peer review processes about the use of AI by referees. This includes some of the journals widely viewed as among the best or most prestigious in philosophy, from which you&#8217;re expecting to hear from fellow humans.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Such gaps&#8212;be they in policy or the communication of policy&#8212;should be fixed immediately. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Of course, one challenge with any policy that restricts the use of AI in this context is enforcement. Will editors have the capacity to catch reviewers who violate their journal&#8217;s or their publisher&#8217;s AI policies, or exploit vague policies?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">And what happens when an author suspects their referee report was drafted by an AI? Many journals have a policy about the finality of editorial decisions. <em>Mind</em>, for instance, <a href="https://academic.oup.com/mind/pages/General_Instructions" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">says</a>: &#8220;The decision of the Editors on the acceptability of any manuscript is final. The Editors will not enter into correspondence with authors on any submission that is not accepted for publication.&#8221; Are reasonably suspicious authors just out of luck? Should they be? </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So there are questions about what journal and publisher policies about the use of AI in peer review should be. There are questions about the enforceability of restrictive rules on AI use. There are questions about what recourse authors should have if they believe a referee report was written by an AI. And there are questions about what we should do in, and how we should be preparing for, the future rushing our way in which writing by AIs is indiscernible from writing by humans.</p>



